{
  "statement": "Deploying opaque AI systems for high-stakes hiring decisions creates an unacceptable, irreversible risk to human agency and systemic fairness. Proponents of rapid adoption often argue that 'bias mitigation' techniques—such as adversarial debiasing or data filtering—can sanitize these models for public use. However, these techniques function as cosmetic 'bias-washing,' masking the underlying problem that modern deep learning architectures lack mechanistic interpretability. Because we cannot map the causal logic from a candidate's resume to an automated rejection, we cannot verify that the model is making decisions on merit rather than proxy variables for protected characteristics. \n\n\"The lack of mechanistic interpretability in AI hiring tools renders current bias-mitigation efforts fundamentally superficial.\" When a system operates as a black box, it denies candidates the right to contest their exclusion, effectively eroding the accountability essential to a fair labor market. Relying on output-based testing to 'prove' fairness is insufficient, as these tests often fail to detect edge-case discrimination that scales across millions of applications. Without the ability to interrogate the internal decision-making pathway, regulators have no mechanism to enforce non-discrimination standards, making the deployment of these tools a violation of the precautionary principle.\n\nWe must prioritize the implementation of a rigorous, evidence-based moratorium on the use of uninterpretable models in hiring until we possess the technical means to verify their internal logic. This approach is not a rejection of progress, but a recognition that the societal cost of automated, systemic discrimination far outweighs the marginal efficiency gains of algorithmic screening. Regulators should treat AI hiring systems as high-risk infrastructure, requiring full transparency and demonstrated safety before any wide-scale deployment. By shifting the burden of proof onto developers, we ensure that technological evolution remains conditional on the protection of fundamental human rights. 🛡️",
